    "turn_text": "when we think about this, you just described the two hats you had to wear, the two organizations that you were in charge of. One of the questions that I've been asked repeatedly, and there's a lot of confusion about, as people consider, they said that it was very interesting that the Russians attacked essentially a US-based company. And the misperception may be that that cyber command can only defend against external non-domestic threats, meaning threats that are offshore. One, is that true? Or can you also monitor the traffic that's within the United States? So for example, something like a solar winds attack or network access can be detected, even though it's being perpetuated from inside the continental borders of the United States.",
