    "turn_idx": 41,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, two thoughts come to mind. And then I think another line of kind of discussion. The first thought that was an extension of our last conversation was we had discussed the vulnerabilities that occur when you go to an increasingly digital world. You open up just a whole host of attack vectors that people can seek to exploit. And one of the things I remember bringing up that has always been very concerning to me is data is not like someone breaking into your house and sealing your TV. And they have like a physical object you used to own now they own it. Maybe you can recover it. You can maybe hold them accountable. One, the threshold, the barrier to entry to actually, you know, causing cyber attacks, either as a state actor or as a terrorist or just a You know, someone in your basement, the barrier to entry is so much lower that a lot more people could be involved in it. But once you've exfiltrated someone's information, once you've stolen their data, it's gone. I mean, you own it. It's on the dark web. It's floating around. And I, you know, at least a half dozen times during my time in uniform and with the federal government receiving a letter in the mail. saying, hey, by the way, you know, someone left hard drives under car. The car was broken into the drives are gone. Your PII, your personal identifiable information was on it. Same thing with the office of personnel and management hack, where a lot of the information had been stored for people's security clearances had been scooped up. So once that information's gone, it's gone. And of course, that's what we're seeing now with super computing with large massive data",
