    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72242.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 55,
    "turn_text": "So something that's been on my mind for a while, and in fact, I'd love to write a little bit about is, and in fact, I kind of did a pseudo thesis at the War College when I was part of the Mahan Scholars Group. We were looking at cyber. It's kind of a new attack vector, and it just struck me you know, when you think about nuclear, when you think about a lot of just conventional deterrence, it's easier. You have, of course, mutually assured destruction when you talk about nuclear and that precludes the use of nuclear. Do you see on the horizon any kind of incentive or equalization at play where, you know, each nation state has so infiltrated the other that it's kind of a look, we've got it, we've got to slow this down, we've got to stop it because right now it has the appearance of It's a free for all and in fact, I'm incentivized to do it because if I can take your intellectual property or if I can know much more about you, why would I not do that?",
